# RAISED 2026-08-04 to FOURTEEN, by the event-handler content/IDL attribute
# fix. The SIXTH instance of the two-writers shape in this lane: an event
# handler lived in dom_on*_src (parse-time, four types only, readable only by
# _fire_handler) AND as whatever setAttribute() stashed on the JS object — a
# STRING, which is precisely what created-node dispatch reads and precisely
# what it can never call. So `createElement` + `setAttribute('onclick', …)`,
# the only way to give a created node an inline handler, wrote to a store no
# dispatch path could reach. Per spec the content attribute and the IDL
# attribute (el.onclick) are ONE piece of state: setting the content attribute
# compiles it into a function with the element as `this` and installs it as the
# IDL attribute. There is now one store ("@H<name>" own property) and one
# reader (an accessor pair on HTMLElement.prototype, 20 handler names).
# 19 is the wider regression fixture: an onmouseover= on a SOURCE element (no
# non-kind type had ANY attribute-handler route), typeof el.onclick read before
# any dispatch, setAttribute of onmouseover/onkeydown on a created node,
